WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.980
The coding round wants code you could ship.

2
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.217
Not clever code.

3
00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:07.565
Clean code, with the right speed, that handles failure.

4
00:00:07.585 --> 00:00:13.065
Three habits: pick the right structure, stream big data, and write for production.

5
00:00:13.085 --> 00:00:15.675
First: pick the right data structure.

6
00:00:15.695 --> 00:00:19.685
To check if a value is in a list, Python reads the whole list.

7
00:00:19.705 --> 00:00:21.635
A set or a dict uses a hash.

8
00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:23.945
The same check takes one step.

9
00:00:23.965 --> 00:00:25.282
Use a dict to count.

10
00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:26.370
A deque for a queue.

11
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:29.220
And a heap for the top few items.

12
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:31.930
Second: the file is bigger than memory.

13
00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.043
Do not load it all.

14
00:00:33.063 --> 00:00:35.890
A generator gives you one row at a time.

15
00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:40.440
Memory stays flat, even for a file of 100 gigabytes.

16
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.825
Third: write it like production code.

17
00:00:42.845 --> 00:00:44.438
A network call can fail.

18
00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:47.685
So retry, and wait longer after each try.

19
00:00:47.705 --> 00:00:49.623
And make the task idempotent.

20
00:00:49.643 --> 00:00:52.570
Run it twice, and the result is the same.

21
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.370
Then add one small test.

22
00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.620
It shows the interviewer you check your own work.

23
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:00.180
Last: how do you make Python faster?

24
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:04.140
For waiting on a network or a disk, use threads or asyncio.

25
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.140
For heavy maths on the CPU, use processes.

26
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.265
The interpreter lock blocks threads there.

27
00:01:10.285 --> 00:01:13.075
Quick recap of Python for data coding.

28
00:01:13.095 --> 00:01:14.237
Hash for lookups.

29
00:01:14.257 --> 00:01:15.900
Generators for big files.

30
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.660
And retries with idempotent tasks.

31
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.780
Now start the first lesson below.
